course: dictionary
lesson: 14503
title: Words from the workshop
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to one place, such as a workshop, a kitchen or a football pitch. When you meet one, the words around it give you clues. «Mia held the ball-peen hammer and tapped the strip of metal flat.» The clues are hammer and metal, so a peen must be part of a hammer.
explain: A hammer has two ends. The flat end strikes the nail, and the other end is called the peen. «She turned the hammer over and used the peen on the rivet.» Here peen is a noun, a thing you can point to on the hammer.
explain: Peen can also be a verb, a doing word. «The metalworker peened the edge to make it hard.» To peen means to hit metal with a hammer to shape or harden it. Check by swapping in hit and shape: the sentence still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: A peen is the rounded or wedge-shaped end of a hammer head, opposite the flat striking face.
fact: A ball-peen hammer has a rounded peen, and it is used for metalwork.
fact: To peen means to hit metal with a hammer to shape or harden it.
fact: Metalworkers peen metal to bend it or to make it harder.
fact: Peen is a rare word in everyday talk, so you will mostly meet it in workshops and design-and-technology lessons.
exercise: What is the peen of a hammer? | The sharp tip of a nail | The handle you hold | The flat face that strikes the nail | *The rounded or wedge-shaped end, opposite the flat striking face
exercise: «The metalworker peened the edge of the strip.» What does peened mean here? | Painted in bright colours | *Hit with a hammer to shape or harden it | Cut into small pieces | Melted over a flame
exercise: Ben says peen is a fancy word for a whole hammer. Why is he wrong? | *Peen is only one part of a hammer, the end opposite the flat face | Peen is a kind of nail | Peen means a box of tools | Peen means the handle and the head together
test: «Sam used the ball-peen hammer to flatten the metal.» What is a ball-peen hammer? | *A hammer with a rounded peen end | A hammer with no head at all | A toy hammer for very young children | A type of saw
test: «Her job is to peen the rivets so the metal joins tightly.» What does peen mean in this sentence? | *To hit metal with a hammer to shape or harden it | To measure how long the rivets are | To paint the rivets gold | To cut the metal into thin strips
exam: What is the difference between peen as a noun and peen as a verb? | *The noun is part of a hammer; the verb is hitting metal with a hammer to shape it | The noun is a tool; the verb is a sound | The noun is a metal; the verb is a colour | They mean exactly the same thing
